Dev log — July 17, 2026, PitWall Day 1

Finally started PitWall for real.

Set up the VS project, SQLite via sqlite3.h, decided on a
domain/repository/service split (repo only knows domain stuff,
storage handles raw SQL, service ties it together). Spent a while
making sure I actually understood the direction of that chain and
wasn't just repeating it.

Built the Node hierarchy — Driver/Team/Circuit as subclasses,
polymorphic via get_type_string(). Went back and forth on what
data actually belongs on a node. First instinct was to throw in
nationality, logos, etc., but caught myself — that's UI/SQL stuff,
not graph stuff. Nodes only hold the math the Markov engine
actually needs (tire_management_modifier, base_pace_delta, etc.).
Killed StrategistNode for now since I don't know what it'd even
hold yet.

Edge struct has winRate, affinityScore, performanceDelta, and
tirePreference. Almost made it an unordered_map keyed on
pair<int, int> — turns out pair has no default hash, would've
broken. Used a regular map instead.

Graph itself keeps both successor and predecessor maps. Felt
redundant at first, but otherwise removing a node means scanning
the entire graph to find incoming edges. Reused this from the
graphs lab I did earlier this year, just adapted it to hold real
Node objects instead of bare ints.

Got it compiling after two dumb mistakes:

- Include path was wrong because Solution Explorer shows folders
  that don't actually exist on disk (VS lets you fake folder
  structure visually). Learned that the hard way.

- Updated DriverNode's constructor to take new fields, then forgot
  to update the make_shared call using it. Compiler complained way
  down in some xutility internals file, which was confusing until
  I realized it was just a constructor mismatch.

Two tests passing: node creation/polymorphism, and edge
add/get/is_edge.

Still missing remove_node/remove_edge. I know exactly how they
should work (predecessor scan, clean both maps, drop from costs
too), just haven't coded them yet. That's next.


Dev Log — July 23, 2026

PitWall — Phases 1-4 completed and verified

What got done:

Fixed DFS (was declared but never implemented) — iterative stack-based traversal
Added remove_node() — cleans registry, adjacency maps, and incident edges
Implemented Dijkstra for shortest-path queries
Built SQLite persistence via Repository pattern (vcpkg-managed, not vendored) — full save/load round-trip, including a tricky ID-gap bug when nodes get removed before saving
Built the data import pipeline: Python script pulls the real 2024 F1 season from the Jolpica API, C++ DataImporter reads it into the graph
10/10 tests passing. Real data loaded: 59 nodes (25 drivers, 10 teams, 24 circuits), 479 edges with computed win rates
Pushed everything to GitHub

Bugs squashed:

MSVC narrowing/aggregate-init errors (C2397/C2512) from sloppy Edge brace-init
Stale debug .db file causing abort() on repeat test runs
Wrong Jolpica API domain (was api.jolpica.com, should've been api.jolpi.ca)
API pagination cutting results off at 100 instead of full season (needed ?limit=1000)

Where it stands: Graph engine + SQLite + real F1 data all working end to end. 
First time PitWall processed actual race data instead of test fixtures.

Next up: Phase 5 — Markov transition model. Scoping down from the full (driver, circuit, lap, tire, position) state space to a (driver, circuit, grid_position) → 
finish_position model, since one season isn't enough data to populate the full space meaningfully.



Dev Log — July 27-29, 2026
PitWall — Markov model (Phase 5) + a real audit

Built the actual Markov layer. Scoped it down hard, on purpose: the
README dreamed of a (driver, circuit, lap, tire, position) state space,
but one season of data can't fill that — most cells would have one
observation or zero. Went with pooled grid → finish instead. Every grid
slot has ~20 real data points, so the distributions actually mean
something.

5a: Trainer counts grid→finish transitions (raw integer counts, normalize
at query time — makes updates trivial). Engine turns a grid position into
a probability distribution. Smell-tested it: pole (P1) predicts a P1 finish
46% of the time. That's real — 2024 was front-runner-dominant. Model
matched reality without me hardcoding anything, which is when I started
trusting it.

5b: driver performance index. Considered per-driver matrices, killed the
idea — same one-season starvation problem, each driver raced each circuit
once. Went with a "handicap" instead: one scalar per driver = average
positions gained/lost (grid - finish). Shifts the pooled distribution.
This finally put real numbers in param2, which had been 0.0 forever.

Then a full audit, and this is where it got good:
- Found the driver index was ranking substitute drivers (Colapinto, Bearman
  — ~5-9 races) above full-season guys like Hamilton. One good drive over
  few races spikes the average. Added a 10-race minimum. Kept it honest
  in the README instead of hiding it.
- Dropped 3 dead edge fields (affinity_score, performance_delta,
  tire_preference) — all 0.0, no feature was ever going to fill them.
  Speculative schema from day 1. Lean schema reads as finished, not
  aspirational.
- The shift was rounding the index to an integer — +1.875 and +1.2
  collapsing to the same shift, throwing away precision I'd computed.
  Switched to fractional shift with mass interpolation between straddling
  positions. Had to watch the "sums to 1.0" invariant like a hawk.

Best moment: a strong driver (Zhou, +2.08) from P5 was showing 31% at P1,
which felt too high. Instead of assuming a bug, derived it by hand — turns
out it's correct. When you shift a P5 distribution forward by 2+, three
buckets land at or below P1, and there's no finish better than first, so
they pile up at the boundary. Not a bug — the model saturating at P1,
which is what "overperformance" should do when you're already near the
front. Documented it as a known limitation. Would've "fixed" correct code
into broken code if I'd trusted the surprising number instead of checking.

Phase 6: StrategyReporter. Deterministic string composition, NO LLM — on
purpose. An LLM can claim things the numbers don't support; a template
can't. It states only what the data backs (positional probability), and
honestly says "no adjustment available" for filtered-out drivers instead
of faking one.

Also rewrote the README, which had drifted into fiction — it promised pit
strategy, tire degradation, and an LLM, none of which exist. Now it says
what PitWall actually is: a grid→finish predictor with documented limits.
Realized the "service layer" I planned on Day 1 was never actually built —
orche


Dev Log — August 3-7, 2026
PitWall — Markov model, the big audit, service layer, and going Bayesian

Massive stretch. Went from "graph engine with data" to an actual honest
forecaster. A lot happened so this is the long one.

Markov model (Phase 5):
Built the actual prediction layer. Scoped it down hard on purpose — the
README dreamed of a (driver, circuit, lap, tire, position) state space but
one season can't fill that, most cells would have one data point or zero.
Went pooled grid -> finish instead. Every grid slot has ~20 real
observations so the numbers actually mean something. Trainer counts
grid->finish transitions (raw integer counts, normalize at query time),
engine turns a grid position into a probability distribution. Smell-tested
it: pole predicts a P1 finish 46% of the time. That's real, 2024 was
front-runner heavy. Model matched reality without me hardcoding anything,
that's when I started trusting it.

Then the driver index (5b). Killed the idea of per-driver matrices — same
one-season starvation problem, each driver raced each circuit once. Went
with a handicap instead: one number per driver = average positions
gained/lost (grid - finish). Shifts the pooled distribution. Finally put
real values into param2 which had been 0.0 forever.

The audit — this is where it got good:
- Driver index was ranking substitutes (Colapinto, Bearman, ~5-9 races)
  above full-season guys like Hamilton. One good drive over few races
  spikes the average. Added a 10-race minimum, documented it honestly in
  the README instead of hiding it.
- Dropped 3 dead edge fields (affinity_score, performance_delta,
  tire_preference), all 0.0, no feature was ever going to fill them.
  Speculative schema from day 1. Lean schema reads as finished.
- The shift was rounding the index to an integer, so +1.875 and +1.2
  collapsed to the same shift, throwing away precision I'd computed.
  Switched to fractional shift with mass interpolation. Had to watch the
  "sums to 1.0" invariant like a madman.

Strategy reporter (Phase 6): deterministic string composition, NO LLM, on
purpose. An LLM can claim things the numbers don't support, a template
can't. States only what the data backs and honestly says "no adjustment
available" for filtered-out drivers instead of faking one. Also rewrote the
README, which had drifted into fiction (promised pit strategy, tire
degradation, an LLM, none of which exist).

Service layer: realized the "service" I planned on day 1 was never actually
built — orchestration was scattered across main and the tests. Extracted a
real PitWallService that owns the whole pipeline, load() does everything,
main became thin. Also split app and tests into two separate build targets
(PitWallEngine.exe + PitWallTests.exe). Caught a tricky one here: two of the
tests used assert(), which gets compiled OUT in Release builds, so they'd
silently pass without testing anything. Converted them to the ok &= pattern
so they actually run in Release.

Championship simulator (Phase 7): Monte Carlo, simulate the rest of the
season from any point, report title odds. Sample-then-rank so no two drivers
"finish P1" in the same race. Validated it from scratch — reproduced the
real 2024 top 4 (Verstappen, Norris, Leclerc, Piastri) from nothing but
average grid positions. Added a clinch/elimination constraint: if a driver
literally can't be caught mathematically, they're 100%, no simulation needed.
This last feature is not as accurate as real life behaviour but it's still
a good assset - for me at least.

The Bayesian rework (the big one):
This is the exact thing i needed and wanted.
Noticed the sim gave Verstappen 88% after only 5 races. That's wrong — after
5 races you know almost nothing. Dug in and found the real problem: the sim
used full-SEASON average grid, so forecasting "from race 5" was secretly
peeking at all 24 races. Future leakage. It wasn't predicting, it was
pretending to.

Fixed it properly with a Dirichlet prior. Give every driver a fake "starter
kit" — pretend they've finished once in every position before the season
starts. Then add their real finishes from races 1..N only, never the future.
Early season the fake counts dominate so nobody looks certain, late season
real data swamps the prior and it sharpens. Bayesian updating, but it's just
counting. Result: Verstappen at race 5 dropped from 88% to 60.7%, with Pérez
and Leclerc holding real chances. Honest uncertainty early, confident late.

One thing I confirmed rather than "fixed": race 20 odds dip slightly
(Verstappen 95.7% at race 15 -> 90.3% at race 20). Checked who holds the
gap — it's Norris at 9.3%. That's correct, not a bug: the model's picking up
McLaren's real late-season surge. A forecaster SHOULD raise a driver's odds
when they're finishing well recently. Left it, documented it.

Where it stands: honest Bayesian championship forecaster, leakage-free,
19 tests green, both build targets clean. 

